{"timestamp":"2026-09-02T10:04:37.795Z","ordinal":18065,"type":"response_item","payload":{"type":"custom_tool_call_output","id":"ctco_01a06193-e623-7a11-ac16-03329b20662a","call_id":"call_BSVvMuT1oGjknHIOzDfiuBki","output":[{"type":"input_text","text":"Script completed\nWall time 0.1 seconds\nOutput:\n"},{"type":"input_text","text":"KV decode, and CPU compression contention rather than isolating one codec\nkernel.\n\nFor a 16-layer KV-only pipeline, CD3's H2D/decode overlap reduced its own\nmakespan by 30.3% at N=256 and 25.8% at N=1024. Against P4Flat's best measured\nsetting, CD3 was 46.7%, 20.8%, and 1.0% faster at N=64/256/1024. The production\nthreshold keeps N<256 on one stream because two-stream event overhead exceeds\noverlap benefit there.\n\n## 9. Retained optimization work\n\n### 9.1 CPU encoder\n\n- Block-owner one-pass encoding: one OpenMP owner completes a KV block in\n  tile/thread/item order, eliminating global prefix buffers, barriers, and the\n  second source-exponent read.\n- Min/max exception-free fast path: if 16 exponents fit in one 3-bit range,\n  candidate search and per-element exception work are skipped.\n- Direct local Packed20x3 construction with uninitialized temporary exception\n  scratch; only valid entries are compacted.\n- NEON extraction of exponent/sign-mantissa and hierarchical packing of 16\n  three-bit codes.\n- SVE2 predicate counting only on the uncommon exception path.\n- CPU weight encoding by independent 64-row stripes, preserving the exact GPU\n  tile order while exposing enough parallel work.\n- Adaptive OpenMP team sizing and worker affinity, especially for an explicit\n  single KV worker.\n- Batched NumPy KV slot assignment instead of a per-block Python loop.\n- Weight encode defaults to CPU. GPU encode remains only behind the explicit\n  debug override `TIERED_SERVE_CD3_WEIGHT_CPU_ENCODE=0`.\n\n### 9.2 Standalone GPU decoder\n\n- Aligned two-`uint32` loading of each six-byte/48-bit code chunk. The final\n  thread reads bytes 1528..1535, so it never crosses a 1536-byte tile.\n- 128 physical threads per tile, each handling two logical 16-value chunks,\n  improving occupancy relative to the original 256-thread layout.\n- Two half-width live output accumulators followed by the same two 128-bit\n  stores, reducing register pressure. This improved aggregate layer-40 weight\n  decode by about 1.4%.\n- 2-D `(tile, block)` KV launch geometry.\n- Direct-to-final-cache indexed KV decode, eliminating the intermediate raw\n  BF16 copy.\n\n### 9.3 KV transport and orchestration\n\n- One aligned slot-major blob containing codes, sign/mantissa, bases, offsets,\n  and Packed20x3 entries, with explicit row strides in CPU/GPU kernels.\n- One `cudaMemcpyBatchAsync` submission for all selected K/V staging runs.\n- Selected-run staging for partial restores instead of copying every slot up\n  to the high-water mark.\n- Cached block/slot GPU metadata and one native paired K/V decode dispatch.\n- The loader publishes the canonical active set once per forward; per-layer\n  decoding then uses identity/version checks with generation-safe invalidation\n  instead of repeating a NumPy content comparison 80 times.\n- Dense steady-state plans that skip filtering, sorting, and contiguous-run\n  reconstruction.\n- Explicit-stream native decode dispatch and a two-slot compressed-blob HBM\n  ring overlap layer L+1 H2D with layer L decode for N>=256. Smaller restores\n  stay on one stream to avoid fixed event/stream overhead.\n- The KV memory planner accounts for CD3's fixed compressed row and reserves\n  one resident partial-tail row per request, preventing host over-allocation\n  and preserving full-block correctness.\n\n### 9.4 Fused Hopper kernel\n\n- Decode directly into WGMMA register A; no dense BF16 weight HBM write.\n- Expand each K tile's Packed20 exceptions into a shared exponent map before\n  WGMMA. This avoids P4Flat-style post-GEMM rank-1 correction for CD3's much\n  larger exception population.\n- Aligned two-`uint32` shared code extraction and register caching of each\n  48-bit code word rather than repeated byte loads.\n- One reusable exception-map stage for TileN<=64 and TileN=128.\n- A two-entry code ring for TileN=64 and a two-entry B ring for TileN=128,\n  reducing shared memory and raising GH200 residency to approximately five and\n  four CTAs respectively.\n- Native TileN=16 and TileN=32 paths to avoid padding small batches to 64.\n- N=48 specialization that reuses one decoded A fragment for standard WGMMA\n  N=32 plus N=16 instructions.\n- Shape/N-specific split-K: split-4 for all production projections through\n  N=64; at N=128, QKV/O/Down use split-4 while GateUp stays split-1.\n- Exact `Npad`: 16, 32, 48, then multiples of 64. This avoids P4Flat's\n  small-M N=128-to-192 widening behavior.\n- Projection-specific fused cutoff overrides and exact-length Packed20x3\n  staging views for calibration and variable exception streams.\n\n### 9.5 Routing and reproducibility\n\n- All four Llama-70B projection shapes are fused through N=128.\n- The standalone/raw prefill cutoff is 1856 and remains overrideable with\n  `TIERED_SERVE_PREFILL_ALT_MAX_CHUNK`.\n- Startup route reporting reflects projection-specific overrides.\n- The current implementation is stored in checksum-verified, idempotent HFS\n  startup patches for tiered-serve and its two required vLLM integration\n  points.\n\n## 10. Investigated but not retained\n\n- Reserving code 7 as a sentinel: invalid for current CD3 and materially worse\n  compression/decode behavior.\n- Removing the exception mask while keeping code 0: incorrect because normal\n  delta 0 and an exception would be indistinguishable.\n- Sparse warp-owner exception pre-application: about 2.3x slower.\n- Full `tCrA` ping-pong/double buffering: register count crossed the occupancy\n  cliff; rejected.\n- Exception-map-only overlap/epoch-map variants: no stable final win and not\n  present in the production kernel.\n- Pairwise standalone three-`uint32` loading: neutral or regressive; retained\n  loader is the aligned two-word loader per 48-bit chunk.\n- 64-thread and register-forced 10-CTA kernels: 7--12% slower.\n- Separate standalone exception kernel: 50.7% slower than integrated patching.\n- Direct-to-persistent Packed20 CPU packing: 3--6% slower at medium/large N.\n- Broad SVE2 rewrites: slower; only targeted predicate counting was retained.\n- Reducing the fixed KV exception capacity from 768 to 384 saved bytes on the\n  sampled Llama KV cache, but was not retained: the observed maximum is not a\n  correctness bound, and there is no overflow-safe variable-row fallback.\n\n## 11. Hardware/model fit and retuning requirements\n\n| Area | Portability | What must be checked elsewhere |\n|:---|:---|:---|\n| Packed20x3 format, block-owner pass, min/max fast path | General CD3/CPU optimization | Verify exception rate and 64x64 divisibility for the new model |\n| NEON packing/extraction | ARM-specific | On Intel/AMD the scalar fallback works, but AVX2/AVX-512 should be implemented to retain encode speed |\n| SVE2 exception counting | ARM SVE2-specific | Confirm SVE2 availability/vector length; otherwise scalar fallback is used |\n| Worker affinity/adaptive OpenMP | Grace CPU/NUMA tuned | Retune worker count, CPU sets, NUMA placement, and OpenMP team size |\n| WGMMA register-A kernel | SM90/Hopper design | Rebuild and profile on H100, B200, or GB200; check instruction support, registers, shared memory, and spills |\n| 5/4 CTA residency and split-K table | GH200-specific tuning | Re-sweep active CTAs and split-K 1/2/4 for each GPU and exact `(M,K,N)` |\n| Projection shape table | Llama-3.1-70B TP=1-specific | Tensor parallelism or another model changes M/K and can invalidate every split/cutoff choice |\n| N=16/32/48/64/128 specializations | Workload-distribution fit | Keep if serving actually spends most decode steps in these N buckets |\n| Exception-map cost | LLM-weight-distribution fit | Measure exceptions for every layer; layer-0 QKV already demonstrates an outlier |\n| KV 2-D/direct-cache kernel | Tiered-serve `[16,8,128]` KV layout fit | Validate block size, head count/head dimension, row strides, and cache index mapping |\n| KV split threshold 256 | GH200 transfer/launch fit | Re-sweep the serial/two-stream crossover; correctness is independent of the value |\n| Fixed exception capacity 768 | Workload safety bound | Profile the target KV exception tail; do not reduce it without an overflow-safe fallback |\n| H2D savings | GH200 unified Grace/LPDDR/HBM fabric fit | Re-measure on PCIe, NVLink, B200, or GB200; byte savings remain, latency crossover changes |\n| Fused cutoff 128 | GH200 + Llama-70B + concurrent H2D fit | Re-sweep fused versus standalone under actual serving contention |\n| Prefill cutoff 1856 | Full `cao-cd3-fused` configuration fit | Re-measure standalone versus raw with the target model, residency map, chunking, and fabric |\n\nThe general algorithmic changes are not overfit: fewer passes, fewer bytes,\nbatched copies, cached plans, and avoiding an intermediate dense KV buffer are\nportable. The numeric policies are deliberately hardware/model fits. The\nhighest-risk values to carry unchanged are split-K, CTA residency assumptions,\nthe four Llama projection shapes, fused N=128, and prefill N=1856.\n\n## 12. Recommended port-validation sequence\n\n1. Measure compression ratio and exception histograms for all target-model\n   layers and real KV blocks.\n2. Measure CPU weight/KV encode with the target ISA. Add AVX2/AVX-512 on x86\n   before judging the ARM-vectorized implementation.\n3. Verify bit-exact resident weight and KV decode, then measure it separately\n   from H2D.\n4. Measure H2D-only with the target CPU/GPU fabric and actual residency split.\n5. Profile registers, shared memory, spills, active CTAs, barrier stalls, and\n   tensor-pipe utilization for N=16/32/48/64/128.\n6. Re-sweep split-K for every production projection shape and tenso