{"timestamp":"2026-09-02T10:04:41.476Z","ordinal":18071,"type":"response_item","payload":{"type":"custom_tool_call_output","id":"ctco_01a06193-f484-7160-a725-4cc73aafe8a2","call_id":"call_JsCOyscpFNDkmx5pm6XU2g8L","output":[{"type":"input_text","text":"Script completed\nWall time 0.0 seconds\nOutput:\n"},{"type":"input_text","text":"# CD3 optimization and P4Flat comparison\n\nDate: 2026-09-01 UTC\n\n## 1. Executive conclusion\n\nThe final CD3 implementation is not uniformly faster than P4Flat in every\nisolated operation. Its advantage comes from combining a denser lossless\nformat with substantially faster CPU encoding, less LPDDR-to-HBM traffic, and\na Hopper WGMMA fused path tuned for the decode batch sizes used by the serving\nworkload.\n\nOn the tested GH200 and Llama-3.1-70B-Instruct configuration:\n\n- Total compressed weight size is about 3.1% smaller: CD3 ratio is about\n  1.368x versus P4Flat 1.326x.\n- The LPDDR-resident portion transferred per layer in frozen-split mode is\n  376.586 MiB for CD3 versus 414.502 MiB for P4Flat, 9.15% fewer bytes.\n- CPU weight encoding of one layer's four fused projections is 14.9x to 28.1x\n  faster across the recorded runs. The latest median-of-five run was\n  128.830 ms versus 3620.297 ms.\n- Steady-state CPU KV encoding is 1.20x to 2.88x faster over N=8..2048.\n- Weight standalone H2D+decode is 4.36% faster overall. The H2D part is 10.89%\n  faster, while isolated resident decode is 1.45% slower.\n- In the latency-critical fused route, the sum of the four layer-40 projection\n  medians is 7.08% faster than P4Flat at N=64 and 14.62% faster at N=128 when\n  each codec uses its production route.\n- KV H2D-only, resident-decode-only, and the complete standalone call are now\n  all faster in CD3. Against current P4Flat's 25,600-byte row, CD3 standalone\n  H2D+decode is 30.6%, 36.0%, 13.8%, and 9.8% faster at\n  N=8/64/256/1024.\n- In a clean final-revision Llama-70B serving run, CD3 reduced median TPOT by\n  8.14% at batch 64 and 7.72% at batch 128. TTFT was 0.43% faster at batch 64\n  and tied within 0.02% at batch 128.\n\n## 2. Test platform and terminology\n\n- GPU/system: NVIDIA GH200, compute capability 9.0, Grace ARM CPU, CUDA 13.2.\n- Model weights: real `meta-llama/Llama-3.1-70B-Instruct`, TP=1, BF16.\n- Representative layer: transformer layer 40 unless otherwise stated.\n- Fused serving weights:\n  - QKV: 10240 x 8192, 160 MiB raw BF16.\n  - O: 8192 x 8192, 128 MiB.\n  - GateUp: 57344 x 8192, 896 MiB.\n  - Down: 8192 x 28672, 448 MiB.\n  - Total per layer: 1632 MiB (1.59375 GiB).\n- `N` is the flattened activation row/token count presented to a linear layer.\n- All retained codec paths are lossless; reported comparisons passed raw BF16\n  bit-exact checks.\n\nThe measurements use the following distinct definitions:\n\n- CPU encode: BF16 input is on the CPU. Time includes production codec CPU\n  work and synchronization, but never a CUDA encode kernel.\n- Resident decode: compressed data is already in GPU HBM. Only the GPU decode\n  kernel is timed.\n- H2D-only: only the LPDDR/pinned-host buffers required for the selected path\n  are copied into preallocated HBM staging. On GH200 this is Grace LPDDR to GPU\n  HBM over NVLink-C2C, not a conventional discrete-GPU PCIe transfer.\n- H2D+decode: the production transfer and decode operation is timed. It is an\n  independent measurement, not necessarily the arithmetic sum of separately\n  measured H2D and decode medians.\n- Fused: compressed weight reconstruction occurs in the register-A WGMMA\n  kernel and no dense BF16 weight is emitted to HBM.\n- Standalone: the full BF16 weight is reconstructed in HBM and then consumed\n  by a separate GEMM.\n- Raw: the full uncompressed BF16 weight is transferred and no codec decode\n  kernel runs.\n\n## 3. Format and memory footprint\n\nCD3 preserves the BF16 sign and mantissa byte verbatim and encodes the exponent\nas a 3-bit delta from one base exponent per 16 values. A 64x64 tile therefore\ncontains 1536 code bytes, 256 base bytes, and a sparse exception stream.\nExceptions use a 20-bit `(12-bit tile position, 8-bit exponent)` entry, packed\nthree entries into each 64-bit word (`Packed20x3`).\n\nCodes 0 through 7 are all normal CD3 deltas. An exception temporarily carries\ncode 0 and is distinguished by its Packed20 position/mask. Consequently code 7\ncannot be used as an exception sentinel without changing the format. The\nEscape7 experiment increased exceptions by 85.6%, slowed decode by 11.11%, and\nwould reduce the expected KV compression ratio to about 1.3347x, close to\nP4Flat's 1.3259x.\n\nFor representative layer-40 weights:\n\n| Metric | CD3 | P4Flat | CD3 change |\n|---|---:|---:|---:|\n| Total compression ratio | 1.368x | 1.326x | 3.1% fewer compressed bytes |\n| Frozen-split LPDDR transfer/layer | 376.586 MiB | 414.502 MiB | 9.15% fewer bytes |\n\nBoth codecs retain sign/mantissa in HBM for frozen-split weights. CD3 also\nretains tile exception offsets in HBM. The table includes all transferred\nmetadata, so auxiliary structures are accounted for in the comparison.\n\nKV is different: offloaded KV does not retain the sign/mantissa half in HBM.\nThe selected K and V compressed slot blobs are transferred in full and decoded\ndirectly into the final GPU KV cache.\n\n## 4. CPU encoding\n\n### 4.1 Weight CPU encode\n\nInput was the four real layer-40 fused BF16 CPU tensors listed above. Each\ncodec ran in a fresh process on NUMA node 0, with one untimed warm-up and five\ntimed repetitions per projection; the table contains medians. No GPU encoder\nwas called.\n\n| Projection | CD3 (ms) | P4Flat (ms) | Speedup |\n|---|---:|---:|---:|\n| QKV | 15.824 | 354.944 | 22.4x |\n| O | 12.710 | 285.589 | 22.5x |\n| GateUp | 79.161 | 1987.135 | 25.1x |\n| Down | 21.135 | 992.629 | 47.0x |\n| Sum of medians | 128.830 | 3620.297 | 28.1x |\n\nAn earlier recorded run was 130.7 ms versus 1951.9 ms (14.9x). CD3 is stable\naround 129--131 ms, while P4Flat CPU time is sensitive to host/OpenMP state.\nThe defensible conclusion is an order-of-magnitude win, observed between\n14.9x and 28.1x, rather than one universal multiplier.\n\nWeight encode is normally an offline/load-time step, so this gain mostly\nreduces initialization/preprocessing time and prevents codec preparation from\nstealing GPU SM cycles; it is not added to token latency.\n\n### 4.2 KV CPU encode\n\nInput was real cached K and V blocks from\n`/root/projects/ats-benchmarks/benchmarks/compress/kv_blocks_cache.pt`, each\nblock shaped `[16,8,128]` BF16. K+V is 64 KiB per block. The production\n`compress_batch_async_from_host` plus `wait_compress_async` path was measured\nwith one compression worker, `OMP=all`, NUMA node 0, one warm-up and 20 timed\niterations. GPU decode was used only after timing to verify bit exactness.\n\n| Blocks N | K+V input | CD3 median | P4Flat median | CD3 speedup |\n|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|\n| 8 | 0.5 MiB | 0.17 ms | 0.49 ms | 2.88x |\n| 64 | 4 MiB | 0.28 ms | 0.55 ms | 1.96x |\n| 128 | 8 MiB | 0.40 ms | 0.60 ms | 1.50x |\n| 256 | 16 MiB | 0.49 ms | 0.68 ms | 1.39x |\n| 1024 | 64 MiB | 1.04 ms | 1.25 ms | 1.20x |\n| 2048 | 128 MiB | 1.99 ms | 2.48 ms | 1.25x |\n\n## 5. Weight standalone H2D/decode comparison\n\nThis used `benchmarks/weight_cpu_h2d_decode.py` with the real layer-40 fused\nweights, CPU encoding for both codecs, 10 warm-ups and 50 samples. Encode and\nsetup were outside CUDA timing. Sign/mantissa stayed in HBM; only the\nLPDDR-side exponent representation was transferred using the same\n`cudaMemcpyBatchAsync` helper for both codecs.\n\n| Sum across QKV/O/GateUp/Down | CD3 | P4Flat | CD3 change |\n|---|---:|---:|---:|\n| H2D-only | 1.2027 ms | 1.3497 ms | 10.89% faster |\n| Resident decode-only | 0.9211 ms | 0.9079 ms | 1.45% slower |\n| Serialized H2D+decode | 2.1286 ms | 2.2256 ms | 4.36% faster |\n\nThus the weight win is a transfer win, not a pure decoder win. CD3's 3-bit\ncodes reduce the LPDDR traffic enough to compensate for a slightly more\nexpensive decoder. Per-projection H2D+decode medians were also all lower for\nCD3: QKV 0.2183 vs 0.2338 ms, O 0.1808 vs 0.1904 ms, GateUp 1.1430 vs\n1.1920 ms, and Down 0.5864 vs 0.6095 ms.\n\n## 6. KV H2D/decode comparison\n\nThe input was the same real K/V block cache as the CPU encode test. Each codec\nand block count ran in a new process with CPU encoding, one worker, 20 warm-ups\nand 100 CUDA-event samples. The table is the final saved run; additional fresh\nprocess rounds reproduced the same ordering. No GPU encoder was called.\n\nThis benchmark constructs a compressor directly and never constructs a\n`DualMemoryLoader`; consequently `load_kv_stream=None` and `decompress_batch`\nuses its standalone one-stream fallback. It is a codec/dispatch microbenchmark,\nnot a full serving-pipeline measurement.\n\n| N | CD3 H2D | P4 H2D | CD3 resident decode | P4 resident decode | CD3 standalone total | P4 standalone total |\n|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|\n| 8 | 0.0166 | 0.0279 | 0.0281 | 0.0455 | 0.0432 | 0.0622 |\n| 64 | 0.0261 | 0.0363 | 0.0282 | 0.0439 | 0.0413 | 0.0645 |\n| 256 | 0.0556 | 0.0670 | 0.0356 | 0.0472 | 0.0791 | 0.0918 |\n| 1024 | 0.1669 | 0.1829 | 0.0687 | 0.0745 | 0.2230 | 0.2471 |\n\nAll values are milliseconds from the final saved run. CD3 reduces the\ncomplete one-stream call by 30.6%, 36.0%, 13.8%, and 9.8% at the four sizes.\nThe fixed dispatch gap was removed by publishing the canonical active set once\nper forward, caching the generation-safe decode plan, and dispatching the\nnative paired decoder on an explicit CUDA stream. Every case was bit-exact.\n\nThe earlier result remembered as N=8 tied and CD3 faster above N=8 was\n0.065/0.065/0.102/0.251 ms for CD3 versus\n0.063/0.070/0.107/0.302 ms for P4Flat. It used P4Flat's legacy raw-safe\n`TIERED_SERVE_KV_BLOB_STRIDE=0`, which gives a 32,768-byte row. Current P4Flat\ndefaults to a tight 25,600-byte row. Re-running legacy stride at N=1024\nreproduced H2D/decode/total = 0.229/0.081/0.300 ms, matching the old\n0.232/0.082/0.302 ms. Thus that ordering was a real result for the old P4Flat\nconfiguration, but it is not the correct comparison against current P4Flat.\n\nThe final raw output is stored in `kv_standalone_final_20260901.log` beside\nthis report. The script's `cpu_encode` field is one setup call in a fresh\nprocess and is not used as the production CPU-encoder comparison; section 4.2\nuses the dedicated steady-state encoder benchmark.\n\n## 7. Fused decode+GEMM comparison\n\nInput weights were the four real layer-40 projections. Activations were seeded\nrandom BF16 `[N,K]` tensors. Sign/mantissa stayed resident in HBM and the\nLPDDR-side metadata was copied before the compute stream ran. L2 was flushed\nfor every sample; results are P50 after 10 warm-ups and 50 iterations. The\nnumbers below sum the four independently measured projection medians, so they\nare a per-layer component estimate, not an end-to-end TPOT result.\n\nCodec encode was setup only and excluded from timing. CD3 setup used its\ndefault CPU weight encoder; the P4Flat benchmark helper prepared its object\nwith the existing GPU encoder. This does not affect the timed H2D/kernel\ncomparison because both produce the production bit-exact format, but it must\nnot be read as an encode comparison.\n\n| N | P4Flat production route | CD3 production route | P4 total | CD3 total | CD3 gain |\n|---:|:---|:---|---:|---:|---:|\n| 32 | all fused | all fused | 2.2400 ms | 2.0096 ms | 10.28% |\n| 48 | all fused | all fused | 2.2539 ms | 2.0574 ms | 8.72% |\n| 64 | all fused | all fused | 2.2644 ms | 2.1041 ms | 7.08% |\n| 128 | QKV standalone, other three fused | all fused | 2.6661 ms | 2.2762 ms | 14.62% |\n\nAt N=128, forcing P4Flat QKV to remain fused gives 2.8080 ms versus CD3\n2.2756 ms, a 18.96% total reduction. The forced fused-kernel-only sums are\n1.5953 ms versus 1.1727 ms, a 26.49% reduction. This forced result is useful\nfor comparing kernels, but the 14.62% production-policy result is the fair\nserving route comparison.\n\n"}],"internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"turn_id":"01a06186-6325-79b3-9454-03c11387a0a9","create_time":1788343481.47653}}}
